{% from 'security-context.yml.j2' import hardened_security_context with context %}
---
# Slice 3 fences the observability namespace, and its question is again different from slices 1
# and 2: prometheus and loki each admit a named set of cross-namespace callers, both sets drawn
# from {{ k8s_namespace }} but NOT identical — prometheus-callers admits monitor-bridge,
# homelab-mcp and homepage, loki-callers admits homelab-mcp only (networkpolicy-prometheus.yaml.j2's and
# networkpolicy-loki.yaml.j2's respective `values:` lists). This probe asserts that a pod in
# {{ k8s_namespace }} carrying none of those labels is refused by both.
#
# THIS JOB RUNS IN {{ k8s_namespace }}, NOT {{ k8s_observability_namespace }} — the single
# easiest thing to get wrong in this slice. The observability baseline
# (networkpolicy-observability.yaml.j2) admits `podSelector: {}`, i.e. every pod in that
# namespace, as an intra-namespace peer. A probe pod placed inside observability would therefore
# NOT be blocked by anything, and it would report the fence broken while the policy is working
# exactly as designed. The label below (netpol-probe-slice3) also deliberately matches none of
# `monitor-bridge`, `homelab-mcp` or `homepage`, so Task 4's per-workload policies do not admit
# it either.
#
# What this probe does NOT cover: the intra-namespace podSelector: {} peer itself (grafana ->
# prometheus/loki/tempo and friends). The readiness gate below proves the four targets are up;
# the intra-namespace path is exercised for real in Task 6 Step 5, by grafana rendering its
# panels — a probe run from inside observability cannot stand in for that without also
# invalidating itself, per the note above.
#
# NO HTTP LIVENESS LEG, for two different reasons per target — worth keeping separate, since
# they are different trust models and someone widening either route later should reason about
# the right one:
#   - grafana has use_authelia: true, so Authelia's forwardauth would answer with a 302 before
#     Traefik ever proxied to the app — a probe would print success without ever contacting the
#     target. That is exactly the slice-1 littlelink trap this role's own header documents.
#   - prometheus's route (claude-otel/templates/prometheus-ingressroute.yaml.j2) carries no
#     Authelia middleware at all; it is gated by ClientIP({{ lan_subnet }}) plus the two node
#     bridge /31s, and that file's own comments record pod-source IPs (10.42/16, always .2+) as
#     deliberately excluded from the match. A pod-sourced probe request would fail the ClientIP
#     match before reaching the app — a different failure mode than grafana's, but the same
#     practical result: a probe here would either 404 at the router or misreport, never
#     exercising the app.
# Either way, the readiness gate in tasks/main.yml (EndpointSlice, not HTTP) replaces the
# liveness leg for this slice, same reasoning as the slice-2 probe's gate.
apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: Job
metadata:
  name: netpol-baseline-probe-slice3
  namespace: {{ k8s_namespace }}
spec:
  # Deliberately BELOW the `kubectl wait --timeout=180s` in tasks/main.yml, not equal to it. The
  # two clocks start at different moments, so equal values make it a coin flip which one fires on
  # a slow cold image pull — and the two failures read completely differently. Lower here means
  # the Job is always the clock that expires first, so the pod is terminal (and its logs final)
  # by the time the wait gives up and the log fetch runs. Costs no wall time either way: with
  # backoffLimit: 0 a failed Job never satisfies --for=condition=complete, so the wait burns its
  # full 180s regardless.
  activeDeadlineSeconds: 120
  backoffLimit: 0
  ttlSecondsAfterFinished: 86400
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: netpol-probe-slice3
        # Admits this pod to the sentinel (speedtest:80) only — see
        # networkpolicy-netpol-sentinel.yaml.j2.
        netpol-probe: control
    spec:
      restartPolicy: Never
      enableServiceLinks: false
      automountServiceAccountToken: false
      containers:
        - name: probe
          image: {{ netpol_baseline_probe_image }}
          command:
            - sh
            - -c
            - |
              # 1. CONTROL — the network and DNS work at all, same control as slices 1-2.
              #    A failure here means this probe proves nothing, rather than meaning either
              #    observability policy failed.
              if ! nc -w 5 -z traefik 80; then
                echo "CONTROL FAILED: cannot reach traefik:80, so nothing below is attributable"
                echo "to the policy. Check DNS and the traefik Service."
                exit 1
              fi
              echo "control ok: traefik:80 reachable"

              # 2. THE ASSERTIONS — cross-namespace, direct pod-to-service must FAIL. Inverted:
              #    success here is the failure case. This pod carries none of the monitor-bridge,
              #    homelab-mcp or homepage labels that prometheus-callers or loki-callers admit
              #    (networkpolicy-prometheus.yaml.j2, networkpolicy-loki.yaml.j2 — the two
              #    policies admit different caller sets, see the header above), so if either
              #    connects, that policy admits more than its documented callers.
              #
              #    Ports (9090, 3100) hardcoded from claude-otel/templates/prometheus.yaml.j2 and
              #    loki.yaml.j2's Service definitions. A WRONG PORT HERE FAILS SILENTLY GREEN,
              #    not loudly: these assertions are inverted, so a port nothing listens on makes
              #    `nc` fail, which is the PASS branch. Re-check both hardcoded ports against
              #    their Service source whenever this file is touched — the readiness gate in
              #    tasks/main.yml checks the Service has ready backends, not that these numbers
              #    are the ports they serve.
              if nc -w 5 -z prometheus.{{ k8s_observability_namespace }} 9090; then
                echo "POLICY NOT ENFORCED: reached prometheus.{{ k8s_observability_namespace }}:9090"
                echo "from a pod that is none of monitor-bridge, homelab-mcp or homepage. Either"
                echo "netpol_baseline_obs_enforced is false, or prometheus-callers"
                echo "(networkpolicy-prometheus.yaml.j2) admits too much."
                exit 1
              fi
              echo "isolated: prometheus.{{ k8s_observability_namespace }}:9090 unreachable from an unlisted caller"

              if nc -w 5 -z loki.{{ k8s_observability_namespace }} 3100; then
                echo "POLICY NOT ENFORCED: reached loki.{{ k8s_observability_namespace }}:3100 from"
                echo "a pod that is not homelab-mcp. Either netpol_baseline_obs_enforced is false,"
                echo "or loki-callers (networkpolicy-loki.yaml.j2) admits too much."
                exit 1
              fi
              echo "isolated: loki.{{ k8s_observability_namespace }}:3100 unreachable from an unlisted caller"
{{ hardened_security_context() }}
          resources:
            requests:
              cpu: 10m
              memory: 16Mi
            limits:
              cpu: 200m
              memory: 64Mi
